  <prompt>
    Take r1's ruling on the ADR-040 inline size-cap (FR-B-15, backlog R5): this task is CONTINGENT on task 001's r1 P4-close reconciliation. If r2 owns it, give the inline payload size cap (`SessionLedgerEntries.cs:50` `InlinePayloadCapBytes = 128*1024`) a clear enforcement HOME as memory/ledger hardening in this gate — a single enforcement point with over-cap payloads spilled to reference (store-before-render preserved). If task 001 rules r1 already closed it, this reduces to a verified no-op with evidence.
  </prompt>

  <role>Senior .NET developer fluent in the ADR-040 session-ledger inline/reference payload model and the ledger entry types.</role>

  <goal>
    The ADR-040 inline size-cap has one clear enforcement home: payloads at/under `InlinePayloadCapBytes` inline; over-cap payloads spill to reference (never lossily truncated), with the cap enforced at a single point rather than scattered. If task 001 determines r1 already owns/closed this, the task is a documented verified no-op citing the r1 evidence.
  </goal>

  <inputs>
    <file purpose="spec">projects/spaarke-ai-architecture-redesign-r2/spec.md</file>
    <file purpose="plan">projects/spaarke-ai-architecture-redesign-r2/plan.md</file>
    <file purpose="reconciliation">projects/spaarke-ai-architecture-redesign-r2/notes/</file>
    <file purpose="module-guidance">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/CLAUDE.md</file>
    <file purpose="bff-governance">.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
    <file purpose="ledger-model">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Models/Ai/Chat/SessionLedgerEntries.cs</file>
  </inputs>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">CONTINGENT on task 001 — do not proceed with an enforcement change until 001 rules r2 owns it; a "r1-closed" ruling makes this a verified no-op.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-040">Over-cap payloads spill to reference, never lossily truncated; store-before-render preserved; no parallel cache.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Single enforcement point for the cap — do not scatter the check.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Cap-enforcement tests are KEEP-path; no `Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;`, no DI-registration tests.</constraint>
    <constraint source="bff-hygiene">Run bff-extensions.md checklist; measure publish-size; no new HIGH CVE; state Placement Justification.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>ai/session-ledger, inline-size-cap, payload-reference-spill, adr-040</topic>
    <files>
      <file>docs/adr/ADR-040-session-ledger.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
    </files>
    <patterns>
      <pattern name="Inline payload cap constant" location="src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Models/Ai/Chat/SessionLedgerEntries.cs:50">InlinePayloadCapBytes = 128*1024 — the cap whose enforcement home this task establishes.</pattern>
      <pattern name="Ledger provenance keying" location="src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Models/Ai/Chat/SessionLedgerEntries.cs:39">BuildOutputKey {bindingId}@t{n} — reference-spilled payloads key here.</pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <context>
    <background>R5 backlog: the inline size cap exists as a constant but its enforcement ownership was contingent on whether r1 P4 closed it. Task 001 reconciles r1 P4-close state; this task acts on that ruling — either hardening the enforcement in r2 or recording a verified no-op.</background>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="001" status="required">r1 P4-close reconciliation — determines whether r2 owns this row.</dependency>
    </dependencies>
    <related-work>
      <item>Sibling contingent rows 075/076 are also gated on task 001.</item>
    </related-work>
  </context>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + contingency check">Load spec FR-B-15 + task 001's reconciliation ruling. If r1 owns/closed it → record a verified no-op with evidence + STOP.</step>
    <step order="1" name="Single enforcement home">If r2 owns it, route the inline/reference decision through one enforcement point against `InlinePayloadCapBytes`.</step>
    <step order="2" name="Reference spill">Over-cap payloads spill to reference (keyed {bindingId}@t{n}); no lossy truncation.</step>
    <step order="3" name="Tests">At-cap inlines; over-cap spills to reference; no truncation. Include NEGATIVE below.</step>
    <step order="4" name="Verify + governance">Build; run tests; measure publish-size; state Placement Justification.</step>
  </steps>

  <tools>
    <tool name="dotnet">Build + run ledger cap tests</tool>
    <tool name="git">Commit on the project branch</tool>
  </tools>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Models/Ai/Chat/ (single cap-enforcement home) — OR a verified no-op note</output>
    <output type="test">tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/ (inline/reference cap tests, if r2 owns it)</output>
    <output type="docs">projects/spaarke-ai-architecture-redesign-r2/notes/ (contingency ruling + evidence)</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">The contingency is resolved against task 001: either an enforcement home is added (r2 owns) OR a verified no-op is recorded with r1 evidence.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">If r2 owns it: the cap is enforced at a SINGLE point; at-cap payloads inline, over-cap payloads spill to reference (asserted by tests).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Over-cap payloads are NEVER lossily truncated — they spill to reference keyed {bindingId}@t{n} (store-before-render preserved).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: an over-cap payload does NOT inline (and is not truncated) — a test asserts it spills to reference.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Publish-size delta measured + reported; no new HIGH CVE; Placement Justification stated (or no-op documented).</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The contingency ruling is a judgment boundary: if task 001's reconciliation is ambiguous about whether r1 closed R5 (CLAUDE.md §6 ambiguous requirements), STOP and surface the ambiguity with the r1 evidence rather than guessing ownership — a wrong call either duplicates r1 work or leaves the cap unenforced.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <notes>
    Completed 2026-07-10 as a VERIFIED NO-OP per task 001's Row 5 disposition (`notes/r1-p4-reconciliation.md`, verified-closed). Independently re-verified (not just re-cited) directly against this worktree: single enforcement function `SessionLedger.CapInlinePayload` (`Models/Ai/Chat/SessionLedgerEntries.cs:84-107`, cap constant at :50), invoked at exactly two ledger write seams — `OutputRouter.RouteAsync` (`OutputRouter.cs:175`, immediately before the ledger append at :207-209, which is itself explicitly store-before-render per ADR-040 D2/D8) and the gate-resume writer in `TypedHandlerResumeExecutor` (`TypedHandlerResumeExecutor.cs:512`, before the append at :535). Ran `OutputRouterTests.cs` directly: 23/23 pass, including the at-cap-inlines-verbatim and over-cap-produces-truncation-marker boundary tests and the email-disposition fail-loud-never-partial test. No code changes made — a second enforcement point would violate this task's own single-enforcement-point constraint and task 001's already-adjudicated ownership ruling. One nuance restated (already adjudicated by task 001, not a fresh escalation): the shipped mechanism is a truncation marker with a 16 KB preview, not a full spill-to-reference of the complete over-cap payload — task 001 already ruled this is what row 5's *ownership* contingency required, with full blob/pointer offload as an explicit, separately-tracked future upgrade path. Full evidence + AC-by-AC disposition in `notes/064-adr-040-size-cap-verified-noop.md`. Quality Gates (Step 9.5) skipped per the documentation-only/no-code-change condition — no diff exists for code-review/adr-check to review.
  </notes>
</task>
